<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Absalon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1128–<a href="/years/1201">1201</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Absalon of Lund, Axel Hvide</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Absalon was a Danish churchman and statesman, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roskilde">Roskilde</a> and later Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lund">Lund</a>, and the chief adviser of King Valdemar I. He led Danish campaigns against the pagan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wends">Wends</a> of the Baltic coast, and in 1168 he and Valdemar stormed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arkona">Arkona</a> on Rügen and destroyed the idol of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/svantevit">Svantevit</a>, forcing the Rani to accept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>. He also founded the castle that grew into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/absalon.jpg" alt="Absalon" width="241" height="450" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Absalons_ligsten.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Absalon was born around 1128 near Sorø on Zealand, the son of Asser Rig, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnate</a> of the Hvide clan from Fjenneslev, and Inger Eriksdotter. He was a kinsman of Archbishop Eskil of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lund">Lund</a>. He grew up at his father's castle alongside his older brother Esbern Snare and the young prince Valdemar, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valdemar-i-of-denmark">Valdemar I of Denmark</a>. During the civil war after the death of Eric III in 1146 he studied theology in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, where he absorbed the Gregorian Reform ideal of the church's independence from monarchical rule, while Esbern fought for Valdemar's claim to the throne.</p><p>He first appears in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxo-grammaticus">Saxo Grammaticus</a>'s chronicle at the end of the civil war, helping to broker the peace between Sweyn III and Valdemar at Odense. He and Valdemar narrowly escaped assassination at Sweyn's banquet at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roskilde">Roskilde</a> in 1157. After Valdemar won the throne, Absalon was elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Roskilde in 1158, the king's reward for Hvide support.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Absalon was Valdemar's close counsellor and the chief promoter of the Danish crusades against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wends">Wends</a>, whose raids had left a third of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> wasted and depopulated by Valdemar's accession. He formed a guardian fleet, built coastal defences and led his first expedition in 1160. The crusades lasted from 1164 for twenty years. In 1168 the fortress at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arkona">Arkona</a> on Rügen, with the sanctuary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/svantevit">Svantevit</a>, was conquered, and the people of Rügen accepted Danish suzerainty and the Christian religion. At Charenza the garrison surrendered, and Absalon had the idol of Rugievit cut down and burned. In 1184 he defeated a Pomeranian fleet off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stralsund">Stralsund</a>.</p><p>Absalon wished to free <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> from entanglement with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> and warned against Valdemar's fealty to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dole">Dole</a> in 1162. In 1167 he was granted the land around Havn and built a castle there, from which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a> grew. He held the first Danish synod at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lund">Lund</a> in 1167 and commissioned Saxo's Gesta Danorum. In 1178, at the pope's order, he became archbishop of Lund, keeping <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roskilde">Roskilde</a> by dispensation. Under Canute VI, from 1182, he was the chief policymaker.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Absalon gave up military command in 1184 and in 1192 made his nephew Peder Sunesen bishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roskilde">Roskilde</a>, while another nephew, Anders Sunesen, became chancellor and later his successor at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lund">Lund</a>. He died at Sorø Abbey on 21 March 1201 and was buried there. He had given <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a> to the bishopric of Roskilde and left his holdings to the abbey. He is regarded as the foremost politician and churchman of 12th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, and his expansion gave Denmark dominion of the Baltic for three generations.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Absalon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the twelfth century the dukes fought Polish rulers, Danish kings and Saxon princes; Danish expeditions under Valdemar I and Bishop <b>Absalon</b> devastated the coast and destroyed the temple fortress of Arkona on Rügen in 1168.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-pomerania">Duchy of Pomerania · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Valdemar I the Great and Bishop <b>Absalon</b>, Danish fleets fought the Wendish pirates of the southern Baltic and in 1168 took Arkona on Rügen, destroying the temple of Svantevit.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-denmark">Kingdom of Denmark · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1168 Arkona on Rugia surrendered to King Valdemar I of Denmark, and Bishop <b>Absalon</b> destroyed the temple of Svetovid.</blockquote><a href="/religion/slavic-paganism">Slavic paganism · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>…the temple-stronghold of the four-headed Svantevit at Arkona on Rügen surrendered to King Valdemar I of Denmark, and Bishop <b>Absalon</b> destroyed the temple; the last place of Slavic paganism on the maps, a holy place at Kobarid in present-day Slovenia, was stamped out in a “crusade” in 1331.</blockquote><a href="/essays/faiths-on-the-move">Faiths on the Move</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1168 King Valdemar I of Denmark and <b>Bishop Absalon</b> stormed Arkona, destroyed the idol and forced the Rani to accept Christianity and Danish overlordship.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-rugen">Principality of Rügen</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1168 King Valdemar I of Denmark and <b>Bishop Absalon</b> captured Arkona and destroyed the idol of Svantevit.</blockquote><a href="/state/rani">Rani</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Fjenneslev, in what is now <a href="/countries/denmark">Denmark</a>. Died in Sorø, in what is now <a href="/countries/denmark">Denmark</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=55.44,11.66&amp;year=1175" aria-label="Fjenneslev on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4805 271 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Absalon"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5255 541h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5255 541h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=55.44,11.66&amp;year=1175">What was here in 1175? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
